<!-- LASTMOD COMES FROM GIT, NOT FROM THE CLOCK AND NOT FROM UPLOAD TIME.
     The fifteen hand-written entries below had drifted to 2026-07-24 (two to
     -07-28) while every one of those pages had taken substantive content commits
     on 2026-08-02/03 — new FAQ answers, corrected ISO sizes on /download, a
     changed Braille claim on /accessibility — so the sitemap was telling
     crawlers that eleven days of real edits had not happened. Corrected to
     2026-08-03, which is what `git log -1` reports as the committer date (%cs)
     on each of the fifteen files, and is the same value the generated guide
     block above carries. (The flag cannot be written out here: a double hyphen
     is illegal inside an XML comment and makes this file unparseable, which is
     worse than a stale date because a malformed sitemap is ignored entirely.)

     Do NOT set these from the HTTP Last-Modified header or from file mtime.
     Last-Modified is deploy time, mtime is checkout time; publish-guide.py's
     _lastmod() docstring records why (a fresh clone would tell a crawler every
     page changed today). Using deploy time here would also have made these
     entries claim to be newer than the guide entries generated from the very
     same commit.

     STILL A MANUAL STEP, AND THAT IS THE OPEN RISK. The guide block below is
     regenerated by tools/publish-guide.py; these fifteen are not, and they have
     now drifted twice. The durable fix is to generate them from git the same
     way — this comment is not a mechanism, which is the lesson recorded in the
     comment further down about the four translations that shipped unlisted. -->
